What Is Driving Demand for Precision Dicing Blades?
Advanced integrated circuits and dense packages leave narrower streets between devices, increasing the importance of kerf control and clean edges. Materials such as silicon carbide, gallium arsenide and certain ceramics are more difficult to cut than ordinary silicon; the wheel must balance cut speed against fracture and wear. Diamond grit exposure, binder stiffness and blade thickness all influence the result.

Consistent repeat purchases depend on qualification and predictable tool life. One factory may select a hub-mounted electroplated blade for a specific thin-wafer process, while another requires a hubless ring or a resin-bonded tool for its package stack. A production line may retain an older blade choice for years if changing it would create unacceptable yield risk.

